# Install Obot Sentry on Windows

## MSI

The MSI sets up Obot Sentry to run automatically. It:

- registers scheduled tasks that scan the device every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes
- installs and maintains the audit hooks — at logon and hourly{{if .enforcementEnabled}}
- installs and maintains the tool-call enforcement hooks (see
  [Tool-call enforcement](#tool-call-enforcement)){{end}}
- handles upgrades and uninstalls out of the box.

Open PowerShell as Administrator and run the installer, replacing
`REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY` with your enrollment key:

### Install

```powershell
msiexec /i "obot-sentry.msi" /qn SERVER_URL="{{.serverURL}}" ENROLLMENT_KEY="REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY" SCAN_INTERVAL_MINUTES="{{.scanIntervalMinutes}}"{{if .enforcementEnabled}} ENFORCEMENT=1{{end}}
```
{{if .enforcementEnabled}}
`ENFORCEMENT=1` writes `EnforcementEnabled` to the registry, which is what the
hook task reads. Keep it in the command you use for **upgrades** too: an install
command without it writes 0 and stops enforcing.
{{end}}
### Trigger first scan immediately (Optional)

To run a scan immediately after install, run the following command:

```powershell
Start-ScheduledTask -TaskName 'Obot Sentry Scan'
```

### Uninstall

```powershell
msiexec /x "obot-sentry.msi" /qn
```

This unregisters the scheduled tasks but leaves the hook configuration the
tasks already wrote into each user's agent files. Remove it with the steps in
[Remove the hooks](#remove-the-hooks) below.

## Standalone binary (Alternate)

Manually install `obot-sentry.exe` to run one-off scans or install hooks
yourself. Use this to try Obot Sentry without the automated scanning and
hook maintenance the MSI sets up.

1. Open PowerShell as Administrator and install the binary:

   ```powershell
   New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry" | Out-Null
   Copy-Item obot-sentry.exe "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe"
   ```

2. Configure Obot Sentry, replacing `REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY` with your
   enrollment key:

   ```powershell
   $key = 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry'
   New-Item -Path $key -Force | Out-Null
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name ServerURL -Value "{{.serverURL}}"
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name EnrollmentKey -Value "REPLACE_WITH_ENROLLMENT_KEY"
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name ScanIntervalMinutes -Value "{{.scanIntervalMinutes}}"
   Set-ItemProperty -Path $key -Name EnforcementEnabled -Type DWord -Value {{if .enforcementEnabled}}1{{else}}0{{end}}
   ```

3. Enroll the device:

   ```powershell
   & "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" enroll
   ```

   Enroll verifies the configuration and prints the device ID this
   machine reports as.

### Run a scan

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" scan --submit
```

By default, scans are submitted at most once every {{.scanIntervalMinutes}} minutes; runs within that window skip submission.

### Install hooks

```powershell
& "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry\obot-sentry.exe" hook-install{{if .enforcementEnabled}} --enforce{{end}}
```

### Uninstall

Remove the hooks (see [Remove the hooks](#remove-the-hooks) below), then remove
the binary, the configuration, and the device identity and scan state:

```powershell
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "C:\Program Files\Obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "$env:ProgramData\obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\obot\obot-sentry" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
```

{{if .enforcementEnabled}}## Tool-call enforcement

Each tool call in Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor is checked against Obot's
allowlist before it runs. The check is a single request to Obot, and it is
**fail-closed**: if Obot cannot be reached, the device is not enrolled, or the
hook cannot tell which MCP server a call targets, the call is blocked.

- Blocking a call never *approves* one. Your agents' own approval prompts still
  apply to everything that is allowed.
- Visual Studio Code is not supported for enforcement. Its audit hook is unaffected.
- An MCP server can be allowlisted by URL, by its `npx`/`uvx` package, or — for a
  claude.ai connector — by display name. A stdio server started from a local
  executable path presents none of those, so it cannot be allowlisted and is
  blocked by design.

Turning enforcement off in Obot stops the blocking immediately — the server
allows every call when enforcement is disabled — even before the hooks
themselves are removed. To turn it off on this device instead, set the registry
value and let the hook task reconverge:

```powershell
Set-ItemProperty -Path 'HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Obot\obot-sentry' -Name EnforcementEnabled -Type DWord -Value 0
```

{{end}}## Remove the hooks

`hook-install` has no uninstall command, so its hook configuration must be
removed by hand — the MSI uninstall and the cleanup above do not touch it.
Every entry it writes is marked with `--managed-by obot-sentry`. Find the files
that still carry one:

```powershell
Get-ChildItem -Recurse -File -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue `
  "$env:USERPROFILE\.claude","$env:USERPROFILE\.copilot", `
  "$env:APPDATA\Code\User","$env:ProgramData\OpenAI\Codex","$env:ProgramData\Cursor" |
  Select-String -List 'managed-by obot-sentry' | Select-Object -ExpandProperty Path
```

Then clean up each agent:

- **Copilot (VS Code) hook** — this file is written only by Obot Sentry, so
  delete it:

  ```powershell
  Remove-Item -Force "$env:USERPROFILE\.copilot\hooks\obot-sentry.json" -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
  ```

- **Claude Code** (`%USERPROFILE%\.claude\settings.json`), **Codex**
  (`%ProgramData%\OpenAI\Codex\requirements.toml`), and **Cursor**
  (`%ProgramData%\Cursor\hooks.json`) — open each file and delete every hook
  entry whose command contains `--managed-by obot-sentry`, leaving any of your
  own hooks in place. The marker is on every entry Obot Sentry writes, including
  the enforcement entries under `PreToolUse`, `beforeMCPExecution`, and
  `preToolUse` — remove those alongside the post-tool ones. In the Codex file Obot
  Sentry also pins two values under `[features]`: `hooks = true`, which you can drop if
  Obot Sentry was the only reason hooks were enabled, and
  `non_prefixed_mcp_tool_names = false`, which you can drop if you want that
  Codex feature available again. The two `%ProgramData%` files are machine-wide, so
  edit them in an Administrator PowerShell.

- **VS Code settings** (`%APPDATA%\Code\User\settings.json`) — under
  `chat.hookFilesLocations`, remove the keys Obot Sentry added
  (`~/.copilot/hooks`, `.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/settings.local.json`,
  `~/.claude/settings.json`); delete the whole `chat.hookFilesLocations` object
  if you had no other entries in it.

The per-user files (`.claude`, `.copilot`, VS Code settings) exist once per
signed-in user, so repeat for each user on a shared machine. Restart Claude
Code, Codex, VS Code, and Cursor afterward to drop the hooks.
